The EU AI Act does not reference “ChatGPT” or “generative AI” by name. Instead, it places such systems in the **“general‑purpose AI models” (GPAI)** category covered by Chapter V, Articles 51‑56.

**Baseline obligations for all GPAI models** (including the models that power consumer chatbots such as ChatGPT) are:
- Preparation of technical documentation;
- Adoption of a copyright‑compliance policy;
- Provision of training‑content summaries.

**Additional obligations apply when a GPAI model is classified as carrying “systemic risk”** under Article 51 (e.g., when it meets thresholds such as a high cumulative compute budget for training). In that case, the provider must also:
- Conduct a formal model evaluation;
- Perform adversarial testing;
- Report incidents to the European Commission;
- Implement specific cybersecurity safeguards.

Thus, under the EU AI Act, generative AI systems like ChatGPT are regulated as general‑purpose AI models, subject to the standard GPAI requirements and, if deemed to pose systemic risk, to the extra risk‑mitigation and reporting duties outlined in Articles 51‑56.
